# The "Invalid argument supplied for foreach()" Trap in Laravel Views

The error message `InvalidArgumentException: Invalid argument supplied for foreach()` is one of the most common stumbling blocks for developers transitioning from plain PHP to the Laravel framework. It usually indicates a mismatch between what your controller is sending to the view and what the Blade template expects to receive. In the specific scenario where you are attempting to list users, the root cause almost always lies in how data is passed from the controller to the view, rather than an issue within the `@foreach` loop itself.

Looking at the controller code provided, there is a critical syntax error that breaks the data flow entirely:

```php
public function users() {
    $users = User::all(); // This works fine
    return View::make('users.index', ['users' => '$users']); // ERROR HERE
}
```

The line `['users' => '$users']` is passing the literal string `"$users"` instead of the array variable `$users`. In PHP, to reference a variable inside an array definition, you must use the `$` sign. By omitting it, Laravel creates a view data key named "users" that holds the value of the text string `"$users"`. When Blade attempts to iterate over this in your view using `@foreach ($users as $user)`, it is essentially trying to loop through the characters of the string `"$users"`. Since a single string does not behave like an iterable collection of objects, PHP throws the "Invalid argument supplied for foreach()" exception before the loop can even begin.

To resolve this, you must ensure the variable reference is correct in the controller:

```php
public function users() {
    $users = User::all();
    return View::make('users.index', ['users' => $users]); // Note the $ sign
}
```

Alternatively, using the newer syntax introduced in Laravel 5.5+, you can pass data directly to the view method without explicitly defining an array:

```php
public function users() {
    return View::make('users.index')->with('users', User::all());
}
```

Or even more idiomatically with the `compact()` helper, which automatically passes variables based on their names in the signature or local scope:

```php
public function users() {
    $users = User::all();
    return view('users.index', compact('users'));
}
```

Once the controller correctly passes the collection of user models, the Blade syntax `@foreach ($users as $user)` will work as expected. Inside the loop, accessing properties like `$user->username` is safe because `$user` represents an instance of the `User` model. The helper function `link_to()` correctly generates an HTML anchor tag pointing to the specific user profile route defined by `/users/{$user->username}`.

It is worth noting that modern Laravel applications often rely on routing conventions rather than hardcoding paths inside views. If you are using resource controllers, the route for listing users might automatically be mapped to `Route::resource('users', UserController::class)`, which creates a standard path like `/users`. The controller method should ideally return a view or a JSON response, but passing data via `view()` is perfectly valid for rendering HTML.

Another subtle pitfall involves eager loading. If you are planning to display related data later in the application, remember that `User::all()` only fetches the user rows from the database. Accessing `$user->username` works fine because it's a direct column, but if you try to access `$user->posts` without using `with('posts')`, you will encounter an N+1 query problem or a missing property error depending on your model relationships. For simple lists like this, a plain query is efficient, but always be mindful of the data you are eager loading to keep performance optimal.

If you find yourself debugging similar issues in the future, check the view dump (by adding `dd($users)` in the controller) to verify exactly what data type and content is being passed. In this case, you would have seen a string instead of an array or collection. Correcting the variable reference in the controller resolves the issue immediately, allowing your list of users to render correctly.
